  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and thunderstorms today with outflow gusts\n   of 25-45 mph and heavy rain rates up to 0.5 inch per hour\n\nAn upper level disturbance and associated cold front tracking \nacross the Northern Rockies has already initiated showers and \nthunderstorms over north-central Idaho this morning, and will \nprogress northeastward across the Continental Divide by mid-\nafternoon (3-4pm MDT). Primary hazards include brief heavy \nrainfall and erratic outflow gusts of 30-50 mph, with a 5 to 10 \npercent probability of severe gusts in northwest Montana and along\nthe Divide. Recent high resolution models also show stronger \ncells capable of hail and near severe winds from Lemhi County \nnortheast through Silver Bow County, most likely between 1pm and \n3pm MDT.\n\nWhile winds will generally subside following the frontal passage, \nelevated gradient winds will persist through sunset across Lemhi \nCounty and southwest Montana. Post-frontal showers will develop \nthis afternoon and continue into Thursday, yielding a 60 to 80 \npercent probability of wetting rain (0.10 inches or greater) \nacross the majority of western Montana and north central Idaho.\n\nThe notable exception to this precipitation is far southwest Montana, \nwhere gusty winds and low relative humidity will drive critical \nfire weather conditions from this afternoon through Thursday \nafternoon. Elevated fire weather conditions will linger into \nFriday due to the slow frontal progression.\n\nLooking ahead, persistent southwesterly flow aloft will dominate \nThursday into Friday as a broad upper trough settles over the \nPacific Northwest. This pattern supports near to slightly below \nnormal temperatures alongside daily scattered afternoon and \nevening convection. North-central Idaho is favored for heavier \naccumulations during this timeframe, with high probabilities (60 \nto 80 percent) of exceeding 0.50 inches.\n\nEnsemble guidance indicates general troughing will persist through \nthe weekend before a more pronounced shortwave ejects across the \nregion on Monday. Approximately 50 percent of ensemble solutions \ndepict a closed low tapping into deeper tropical moisture, which \naligns with current NBM probabilities of 50 to 70 percent for \nwidespread precipitation exceeding 0.25 inches to start the next \nwork week across a large swath of the Northern Rockies.",
